      "title": "E53 N62 Blue Smoke: Confirm Valve-Stem Seals Before Repair",
      "description": "Blue exhaust smoke after an extended idle or deceleration and elevated oil use on a 2004-2006 E53 4.4i/4.8is can be consistent with worn N62 valve-stem seals, but those symptoms are not conclusive by themselves. Crankcase-ventilation faults, external oil leaks, cylinder wear, valve-guide wear, or other oil-entry paths must be ruled out. The previous article incorrectly combined the M62 and N62 and suggested generic oil and filter purchases instead of the actual repair parts.",
      "solution": "Measure oil consumption under consistent conditions, inspect for external leaks and crankcase-ventilation faults, check intake contamination, evaluate spark plugs and cylinders, and have a BMW-capable shop confirm the valve-stem-seal diagnosis. If the N62 seals are confirmed and BMW ETK/AIR selects 11-34-0-029-751 for the VIN, the 32-valve V8 requires two 16-seal kits, one for each bank. Use the BMW repair procedure and special tools, replace only the additional seals and one-time-use hardware identified by the VIN-specific diagram, then verify compression, oil use, and smoke after repair. Oil or an oil filter does not repair hardened valve-stem seals.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "confidence": "medium",
